### Workflow Rules

#### When to Check These Rules
- Before starting any new task
- When making changes to code
- Before committing changes
- When creating new files
- When responding to user requests
- When handling sensitive data
- Before deploying to production

#### Must-Check Items
- [ ] Git status checked
- [ ] All modified files reviewed
- [ ] Commit message follows format
- [ ] Changes verified after commit
- [ ] Relevant rule files reviewed for the task
- [ ] No sensitive data is committed
- [ ] Tests are passing
- [ ] Code review completed if required

#### Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Skipping git status check
- Not reviewing all modified files
- Committing without confirmation
- Not following the step-by-step process
- Not checking relevant rules before starting a task
- Using vague commit messages
- Including multiple unrelated changes in one commit
- Committing sensitive data
- Skipping code review when required

#### Step-by-Step Process

##### Before Making Changes
1. Check git status
2. Review relevant rule files
3. List files to be modified
4. Get confirmation before proceeding
5. Check for sensitive data handling requirements

##### During Changes
1. Follow established patterns
2. Document rationale for deviations
3. Review existing code before adding new patterns
4. Handle sensitive data appropriately
5. Write tests for new functionality

##### After Making Changes
1. Check git status
2. List all modified files
3. Verify no sensitive data is included
4. Run tests
5. Propose commit message
6. Get confirmation
7. Execute commit
8. Verify success

#### Git Commit Guidelines

### Git Command Rules
- Non-destructive read-only commands (git status, cat, ls, etc.) can be executed automatically without approval
- Any command that modifies files, stages changes, or commits requires explicit approval
- Always explain the purpose of the command before executing it
- Always add all modified files when committing changes
- Never selectively add files unless explicitly requested
- Use `git add .` as the default command for staging changes
- Only use selective file addition when specifically directed by the user
- Document any exceptions to this rule in the commit message

##### Message Format
- Format: `[Verb] [Brief description]` for each distinct action
- Standard Verbs: Add, Fix, Update, Remove, Refactor
- Description: Clear, concise, present tense
- Use git diff to determine the changes and summarize each distinct action
- Each file change or major functional change should have its own action message
- Multiple actions should be combined into a single line using commas
- Order actions by their impact (major changes first)

##### Example Messages
✅ Correct:
```
[Add] Login feature, password reset functionality
[Fix] Button alignment in login form
[Refactor] Authentication service to use JWT
[Update] User profile form validation
[Remove] Deprecated API endpoint
```

❌ Incorrect:
- "Fixed stuff"
- "Updated things"
- "Changes"
- "WIP"
- "More changes"
- "Fix security issue" (too vague)
- Using newlines in commit messages
- Using \n for line breaks

##### Multiple Action Guidelines
- Review git diff to identify distinct changes
- Group related changes under a single action when appropriate
- Separate unrelated changes into different actions
- Consider the impact and scope of each change
- Use the most specific verb that describes the change
- Maintain chronological order of changes when logical
- Combine multiple actions into a single line using commas

##### Example of Multiple Actions
```
[Add] New user profile page, profile image upload functionality
[Refactor] User model to support profile images
[Update] User controller to handle image uploads
[Fix] Image validation in profile form
[Remove] Old profile image storage method
```

#### Sensitive Data Handling

##### What to Check For
- API keys and secrets
- Passwords and credentials
- Personal identifiable information
- Financial data
- Environment variables
- Configuration files

##### Best Practices
- Use environment variables
- Never commit .env files
- Use secure credential storage
- Follow data encryption standards
- Implement proper access controls
- Log sensitive data access

#### Examples of Good Workflow

✅ Correct:
- "Let me check the git status first"
- "I see we modified these files: [list]"
- "I suggest this commit message: [message]"
- "Before proceeding, I should check the [specific] rules"
- "I'll verify there's no sensitive data in these changes"
- "Let me run the tests before committing"

❌ Incorrect:
- Jumping straight to commit
- Not checking all modified files
- Skipping confirmation steps
- Not reviewing relevant rules
- Ignoring sensitive data checks
- Skipping test runs

